Split out of karr #144 (docs/adr/0044), which fixed that ticket's FIRST direction (the AAD is Go's re-format of the timestamp, not the document text) and left this one open because it cannot be implemented where that fix lives.

MEASURED, sops 3.13.3, on a document written by File::SOPS->encrypt with only the lastmodified line unquoted:

  lastmodified: "2026-08-21T09:05:08Z"   sops -d exit 0   File::SOPS ok
  lastmodified: 2026-08-21T09:05:08Z     sops -d exit 1   File::SOPS ok
        decoding failed due to the following error(s):
        'lastmodified' expected type 'string', got unconvertible type 'time.Time'

go-yaml v3 resolves a bare RFC3339 scalar as !!timestamp, and mapstructure wants a string. We accept it. PERMISSIVE - nothing is decrypted wrongly, and this library never WRITES such a document: File::SOPS::Format::YAML quotes the timestamp on the way out and t/06-wire-format-regressions.t pins that.

WHY IT IS NOT IN METADATA.PM: from_hash sees a Perl string either way. ADR 0038 measured that a bare and a quoted YAML scalar arrive as the SAME Perl string, so no decision in Metadata.pm can tell them apart. The distinction lives in the source text, which is the format handler's - the same cross-phase plain/quoted state ADR 0038 named when it handed karr #135 over, and which _restore_plain_infinities already carries for one token family.

NOTE, since ADR 0044 widened it by one family: a document that is BOTH unquoted AND non-canonical (bare lastmodified: 2026-08-21T09:05:08+00:00) used to fail here for the AAD reason while sops refused it for the resolver reason. It now reads here and is still refused there.

BEFORE IMPLEMENTING: check whether a refusal would hit documents sops accepts. That is exactly why karr #145 was closed as documented-not-fixed, and it is the same question here - a bare scalar that is not a timestamp at all must keep working.

t/58-lastmodified-is-re-formatted-before-it-is-the-aad.t already pins both halves of the divergence, gated on the binary.

Lane: format.

DONE -- implemented as a WARNING, docs/adr/0050, t/64-a-plain-lastmodified-is-warned-about-not-refused.t.

DOES THE ADR 0026 MECHANISM CARRY? No, and that is measured. ADR 0026 asks
YAML::PP as a RESOLVER, and for .inf that answer IS the plain/quoted answer
(Core schema: plain .inf -> float, quoted -> string). YAML 1.2's Core schema has
no timestamp type, so YAML::PP hands back the IDENTICAL Perl string for
`lastmodified: 2026-08-21T09:05:08Z` and the quoted form. What carries is the
QUESTION. YAML::PP's PARSER answers it directly -- the scalar event's style --
which is the oracle _plain_boolean_tagged_scalars (ADR 0030) and
_merge_tagged_scalars (ADR 0028) already use in this file. Measured exact over
17 spellings, including three a regex would miss: a plain scalar on the line
BELOW its key, inside a flow mapping, and behind an explicit `? key`.

DO WE REFUSE SOMETHING SOPS ACCEPTS? No -- measured, not assumed, and it is also
why this is not a refusal at all.
  * All 15 RFC3339 spellings sops reads QUOTED (exit 0) are refused BARE
    (exit 1, unconvertible type 'time.Time'). There is no accepted bare
    spelling.
  * ONE trap found: `!!str <bare timestamp>` is style-plain and sops reads it at
    exit 0, because an explicit tag stops go-yaml's implicit resolver before
    parseTimestamp. A guard on style alone would have fired on a document sops
    opens -- exactly karr #145's condition. Hence the tag check.
  * A plain scalar go-yaml does NOT resolve as a timestamp (hello, 2026, true,
    3.14, null, ~, `...T09:05:08` with no zone) is left alone: mapstructure
    decodes weakly and turns int/float/bool/null into strings, so sops refuses
    all of those at time.Parse whether they are quoted or not. Quoting is not
    the discriminator there, so it is not this family -- it is ADR 0044's
    pass-through.
  * _go_timestamp vs go-yaml's parseTimestamp disagree on 2 of 29 spellings,
    both in the safe direction: `,123Z` (a comma fraction) is a timestamp to
    go-yaml and not to the model -> quiet on a document sops refuses; `+25:00`
    is the other way round -> fires on a document sops refuses anyway.

WHY A WARNING AND NOT A REFUSAL. Measured: rotate on such a document produces
one `sops -d` reads at exit 0, and so does decrypt_file + encrypt_file, because
every write path re-stamps and quotes the timestamp. A refusal would take the
one tool that can still repair the file and leave it openable by nothing --
which in a secrets library is worse than the permissive gap it closes. Same
shape as ADR 0018 and ADR 0019.

Nothing on the wire moves, no digest changes, no document is read differently.
t/58's third subtest inverted: 'sops refuses it, we read it' -> '... and say
so'. Net: 32 assertions across 4 of its 12 subtests fall without the mechanism.
